var documenterSearchIndex = {"docs":
[{"category":"section","location":"api/#API-reference","page":"API","text":"","title":"API reference"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Public-API","page":"API","text":"","title":"Public API"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Internals","page":"API","text":"Not exported. These may change between releases, but are useful when writing new estimators, data sources or sinks.","title":"Internals"},{"category":"module","location":"api/#Vardr.Vardr","page":"API","text":"Vardr\n\nA general-purpose framework for building digital twins on top of SciML.\n\nA digital twin couples a mechanistic model (any ODEProblem, including those generated by ModelingToolkit) with a probabilistic belief about the real system's state, keeps that belief synchronized with incoming observations (data assimilation), and answers forecast and what-if questions.\n\nSee TwinModel, ObservationModel, Twin.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Vardr.Vardr"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#Vardr.AbstractBelief","page":"API","text":"abstract type AbstractBelief\n\nA probabilistic description of the twin's (augmented) state z = [u; θ], where u are the model states and θ are the parameters being estimated online.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Vardr.AbstractBelief"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#Vardr.AbstractDataSource","page":"API","text":"abstract type AbstractDataSource\n\nA stream of Observations feeding a running twin (see run!).\n\nTo add a source, subtype AbstractDataSource and implement Vardr.start(src)::Channel{Observation}. If the source creates that channel itself, also define Vardr.owns_channel(src) = true so the runner can close it on stop!.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Vardr.AbstractDataSource"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#Vardr.AbstractEstimator","page":"API","text":"abstract type AbstractEstimator\n\nStrategy for synchronizing a twin's belief with observations. Every estimator represents the belief by N members that are propagated through the model.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Vardr.AbstractEstimator"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#Vardr.AbstractIntervention","page":"API","text":"abstract type AbstractIntervention\n\nA change applied to the system at a given model time, used for what-if scenarios.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Vardr.AbstractIntervention"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#Vardr.AbstractSink","page":"API","text":"abstract type AbstractSink\n\nReceives the twin after every update made by run!.\n\nTo add a sink, subtype AbstractSink, implement on_update, and optionally Base.close(sink), which is called when the run ends.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Vardr.AbstractSink"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#Vardr.CallbackSink","page":"API","text":"struct CallbackSink{F} <: AbstractSink\n\nCalls f(twin, obs) after every update.\n\nf::Any\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Vardr.CallbackSink"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#Vardr.ChannelSource","page":"API","text":"struct ChannelSource <: AbstractDataSource\n\nObservations pushed by user code: put!(src.channel, Observation(t, y)).\n\nChannelSource(; size = 1024)\nChannelSource(channel::Channel{Observation})\n\nchannel::Channel{Observation}\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Vardr.ChannelSource"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#Vardr.EnKF","page":"API","text":"struct EnKF{L} <: AbstractEstimator\n\nStochastic ensemble Kalman filter (perturbed observations).\n\nEnKF(; N = 50, inflation = 1.0, localization = nothing)\n\nKeywords\n\nN: ensemble size.\ninflation: multiplicative inflation of the ensemble anomalies before each update (values slightly above 1, such as 1.02, counteract ensemble collapse).\nlocalization: Schur-product covariance localization for the model states, given as a tuple (Lxy, Lyy). Lxy (nu × ny) tapers the state–observation covariance and Lyy (ny × ny) the observation–observation covariance, where nu is the number of model states and ny the number of observed outputs. Both must come from the same correlation function, so that the state gain is the exact Kalman gain of the localized covariance. Tapering only Lxy can inflate the spread at the sensors and make the filter diverge. If the observation model observes states directly (ObservationModel(model, vars, σ)), passing Lxy alone is enough: Lyy is then the rows of Lxy at the observed states.\nEstimated parameters are global quantities, so they are always updated with the unlocalized gain.\n\nN::Int64\ninflation::Float64\nlocalization::Any\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Vardr.EnKF"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#Vardr.EnsembleBelief","page":"API","text":"mutable struct EnsembleBelief <: AbstractBelief\n\nEqually weighted sample of the augmented state; column i of X is member i.\n\nX::Matrix{Float64}: Members as columns.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Vardr.EnsembleBelief"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#Vardr.Event","page":"API","text":"struct Event{F} <: AbstractIntervention\n\nRun an arbitrary affect!(integrator) at time t, for anything that SetParameter and SetState cannot express.\n\nt::Float64\naffect!::Any\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Vardr.Event"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#Vardr.NoAssimilation","page":"API","text":"struct NoAssimilation <: AbstractEstimator\n\nOpen-loop twin: members are propagated but observations are ignored.\n\nN::Int64: Ensemble size.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Vardr.NoAssimilation"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#Vardr.Observation","page":"API","text":"struct Observation\n\nA single measurement of the real system at model time t.\n\nEntries of y that are NaN are treated as missing, so a partial sensor reading can still be assimilated.\n\nObservation(t, y)\n\nt::Float64: Model time of the measurement.\ny::Vector{Float64}: Measured values, in the order of the observation model's outputs.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Vardr.Observation"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#Vardr.ObservationModel","page":"API","text":"struct ObservationModel{H}\n\nMaps a model state to what the sensors measure: y = h(u, p, t) + ε, with ε ~ N(0, R).\n\nConstructors\n\nObservationModel(model, vars, σ)\nObservationModel(h, R; names)\n\nThe first form observes model states directly. vars is a vector of state names or indices, and σ is a scalar or a vector of measurement standard deviations. The second form takes an arbitrary function h(u, p, t) -> Vector and a measurement covariance matrix R (or a vector of standard deviations).\n\nh::Any: Measurement function h(u, p, t).\nR::Matrix{Float64}: Measurement noise covariance.\nnames::Vector{Symbol}: Names of the measured outputs.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Vardr.ObservationModel"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#Vardr.ParticleBelief","page":"API","text":"mutable struct ParticleBelief <: AbstractBelief\n\nWeighted sample of the augmented state.\n\nX::Matrix{Float64}: Particles as columns.\nlogw::Vector{Float64}: Log importance weights (unnormalized).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Vardr.ParticleBelief"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#Vardr.ParticleFilter","page":"API","text":"struct ParticleFilter <: AbstractEstimator\n\nBootstrap (sequential importance resampling) particle filter. Suited to strongly nonlinear or non-Gaussian problems; needs more members than the EnKF.\n\nParticleFilter(; N = 500, resample_threshold = 0.5, jitter = 0.0)\n\nKeywords\n\nN: number of particles.\nresample_threshold: resample when the effective sample size drops below this fraction of N.\njitter: after resampling, perturb particles by this fraction of the posterior standard deviation (regularization against sample impoverishment).\n\nN::Int64\nresample_threshold::Float64\njitter::Float64\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Vardr.ParticleFilter"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#Vardr.SetParameter","page":"API","text":"struct SetParameter <: AbstractIntervention\n\nSet one or more parameters at time t, e.g. SetParameter(10.0, :β => 0.1).\n\nt::Float64\nchanges::Vector{Pair{Any, Float64}}\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Vardr.SetParameter"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#Vardr.SetState","page":"API","text":"struct SetState <: AbstractIntervention\n\nSet one or more states at time t, e.g. SetState(5.0, :x => 0.0).\n\nt::Float64\nchanges::Vector{Pair{Any, Float64}}\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Vardr.SetState"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#Vardr.SnapshotSink","page":"API","text":"mutable struct SnapshotSink <: AbstractSink\n\nWrites a snapshot to path every every updates and when the run ends.\n\nSnapshotSink(path; every = 10)\n\npath::String\nevery::Int64\ncount::Int64\ntwin::Any\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Vardr.SnapshotSink"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#Vardr.TableSource","page":"API","text":"struct TableSource <: AbstractDataSource\n\nReplays a table (anything Tables.jl-compatible, such as a DataFrame or CSV.File) as a stream of observations, sorted by time.\n\nTableSource(table; time = :t, columns = nothing, speed = Inf)\n\ncolumns: the measured columns, in the order of the observation model's outputs (default: every column except time). missing becomes NaN, i.e. unobserved.\nspeed: model time units per wall-clock second. Inf replays as fast as possible; a finite value paces the replay to simulate a live feed.\n\nobservations::Vector{Observation}\nspeed::Float64\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Vardr.TableSource"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#Vardr.Twin","page":"API","text":"mutable struct Twin{M<:TwinModel, O, E<:AbstractEstimator, B<:AbstractBelief, P, R<:Random.AbstractRNG}\n\nA digital twin: a model, a belief about the real system's state, and the machinery to keep that belief synchronized with observations.\n\nTwin(model, obs = nothing; estimator = EnKF(), estimate = [], prior = nothing,\n     t0 = model.prob.tspan[1], process_noise = 0, bounds = nothing,\n     rng = Random.default_rng(), name = \"twin\")\n\nKeywords\n\nestimate: parameters to estimate jointly with the states (augmented state z = [u; θ]).\nprior: initial belief over z. Either a Distributions multivariate distribution, a matrix whose columns are members, or nothing for a Gaussian with 10% relative spread around the model's nominal u0 and parameters.\nprocess_noise: model error, as standard deviation per unit time of each component of z (scalar or vector). Applied as a random walk during predict!. A small value on estimated parameters keeps their ensemble from collapsing.\nbounds: (lower, upper) vectors over z. Members are clamped after every prediction and update, for example to keep concentrations non-negative.\n\nname::String\nmodel::TwinModel\nobs::Any: Observation model, or nothing for a twin that is only simulated.\nestimator::AbstractEstimator\nbelief::AbstractBelief\nt::Float64: Current model time of the belief.\nestimated::Vector{Any}: Parameters estimated online (appended to the state).\np::Any: Parameter object shared by all members (estimated entries are overridden per member).\nprocess_noise::Vector{Float64}\nlower::Vector{Float64}\nupper::Vector{Float64}\nhistory::Vardr.History\nrng::Random.AbstractRNG\nlock::ReentrantLock\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Vardr.Twin"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#Vardr.TwinForecast","page":"API","text":"struct TwinForecast\n\nProbabilistic trajectory of the twin's states produced by forecast or scenario.\n\nmean(fc), quantile(fc, q), std(fc): nstates × length(fc.t) summaries\nfc[:x]: length(fc.t) × nsamples matrix of sample trajectories of state x\n\nname::String\nt::Vector{Float64}: Output times.\nnames::Vector{Symbol}: State names.\nsamples::Array{Float64, 3}: Sample trajectories, nstates × length(t) × nsamples.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Vardr.TwinForecast"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#Vardr.TwinModel","page":"API","text":"struct TwinModel{P<:SciMLBase.ODEProblem, A, K}\n\nThe mechanistic core of a twin: an ODEProblem plus the solver used to advance it.\n\nAny ODEProblem works, including one built from a ModelingToolkit system (ODEProblem(sys, u0map, tspan, pmap)), in which case states and parameters can be referred to by their symbolic names.\n\nConstructors\n\nTwinModel(prob::ODEProblem; alg = Tsit5(), solve_kwargs...)\nTwinModel(f, u0, p; states = nothing, params = nothing, t0 = 0.0, alg = Tsit5(), solve_kwargs...)\n\nThe second form wraps an out-of-place right-hand side f(u, p, t) (or in-place f(du, u, p, t)). Pass states/params as vectors of Symbols to enable name-based indexing without ModelingToolkit.\n\nprob::SciMLBase.ODEProblem: Template problem; its u0, p and tspan[1] are the nominal initial conditions.\nalg::Any: ODE solver algorithm.\nsolve_kwargs::Any: Extra keyword arguments forwarded to solve.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Vardr.TwinModel"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#Vardr.TwinRunner","page":"API","text":"mutable struct TwinRunner{T<:Twin}\n\nHandle to a twin running on a data stream, returned by run!. Use wait(runner) to block until it finishes and stop! to end it.\n\ntwin::Twin\ntask::Task\ninbox::Channel{Any}\nsource_channel::Channel{Observation}\nowns_source::Bool\nassimilated::Int64: Observations assimilated.\nlate::Int64: Observations dropped because they arrived after the twin had moved past them.\nticks::Int64: Clock ticks processed.\nrollbacks::Int64: Times the twin was rolled back over provisional ticks to accept delayed data.\ncheckpoint::Any: Belief, time and history length before the current run of ticks (or nothing).\nfailed::Int64: Observations that raised an error during assimilation.\nlast_activity::Float64\nstopping::Bool\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Vardr.TwinRunner"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#Vardr.WallClock","page":"API","text":"struct WallClock\n\nKeeps a running twin advancing through data outages. Whenever interval wall-clock seconds pass with no observation, the twin predicts dt model time units ahead. Set dt to roughly the model time that elapses in interval seconds of the real system.\n\nTicks are provisional. Before the first tick of a quiet period the runner checkpoints the belief. If an observation then arrives that the ticks have already moved past, the twin rolls back to the checkpoint and assimilates it, so data is never dropped because of a tick. The rolled-back tick records are removed from the history.\n\ninterval::Float64\ndt::Float64\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Vardr.WallClock"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#CommonSolve.step!-Tuple{Twin, Observation}","page":"API","text":"step!(twin, obs::Observation)\n\nPredict up to obs.t, then assimilate obs.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CommonSolve.step!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Vardr.assimilate!-Tuple{Twin, Observation}","page":"API","text":"assimilate!(twin, obs::Observation)\n\nUpdate the twin's belief with an observation taken at the twin's current time (obs.t == twin.t). Use step! to predict and assimilate in one call.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Vardr.assimilate!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Vardr.calibrate!-Tuple{Twin, AbstractVector{Observation}, Any}","page":"API","text":"calibrate!(twin, data, params; alg, lb = nothing, ub = nothing, adtype = SciMLBase.NoAD(), u0 = state(twin), solve_kwargs...)\n\nFit model parameters params (names or indices) to a batch of observations by maximizing the Gaussian likelihood under the twin's observation model, simulating from u0 at twin.t. All observations must be at or after twin.t.\n\nalg is any Optimization.jl optimizer, e.g. NelderMead() after using OptimizationOptimJL. Gradient-based optimizers need an adtype such as AutoForwardDiff().\n\nOn success the fitted values replace the twin's fixed parameters. If a parameter is also estimated online, its member values are shifted so that their mean equals the fitted value. Returns the Optimization.jl solution.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Vardr.calibrate!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Vardr.compare-Tuple{Vararg{TwinForecast}}","page":"API","text":"compare(forecasts...; var, t = nothing, quantiles = (0.05, 0.95))\n\nSummarize state var at time t (default: the final time) for each forecast. Returns a Tables.jl-compatible named tuple of columns scenario, mean, std, lower and upper.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Vardr.compare"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Vardr.forecast-Tuple{Twin, Real}","page":"API","text":"forecast(twin, tend; saveat = range(twin.t, tend; length = 101), nsamples, interventions = [], name = \"forecast\", rng)\n\nPropagate samples from the twin's current belief to tend without changing the twin. Returns a TwinForecast. nsamples defaults to the number of members. Each sample's estimated parameters are those of the member it was drawn from, so parameter uncertainty is carried into the forecast. Process noise is not added.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Vardr.forecast"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Vardr.history-Tuple{Twin}","page":"API","text":"The twin's History.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Vardr.history"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Vardr.members-Tuple{Twin}","page":"API","text":"Matrix of belief members (columns) over the augmented state.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Vardr.members"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Vardr.nstates-Tuple{TwinModel}","page":"API","text":"Number of state variables of the model.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Vardr.nstates"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#Vardr.on_update","page":"API","text":"on_update(sink, twin, obs)\n\nCalled after the running twin assimilates obs, or after a clock tick (then obs is nothing).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Vardr.on_update"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Vardr.paramnames-Tuple{TwinModel}","page":"API","text":"Parameter names of the model (empty when the model is unnamed).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Vardr.paramnames"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Vardr.paramvalues-Tuple{Twin}","page":"API","text":"paramvalues(twin)\n\nCurrent parameter values as name => value pairs: posterior means for estimated parameters, fixed values otherwise.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Vardr.paramvalues"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Vardr.predict!-Tuple{Twin, Real}","page":"API","text":"predict!(twin, t)\n\nAdvance every member of the twin's belief from twin.t to model time t, adding process noise. Members are integrated in parallel across Julia threads.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Vardr.predict!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Vardr.restore!-Tuple{Twin, AbstractString}","page":"API","text":"restore!(twin, path)\n\nLoad a snapshot into a twin built from the same model, observation model and estimator. The twin continues exactly where the snapshot left off.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Vardr.restore!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Vardr.run!-Tuple{Twin, AbstractDataSource}","page":"API","text":"run!(twin, source; sinks = [], until = Inf, clock = nothing, on_error = :stop, async = false)\n\nKeep twin synchronized with a live AbstractDataSource: every incoming observation is assimilated with step!, then passed to each sink.\n\nObservations older than the twin's current time (late or out of order) are dropped and counted in runner.late, unless only provisional clock ticks have moved the twin past them (see WallClock).\nuntil: stop once model time reaches this value.\nclock: a WallClock that advances the twin when no data arrives for a while.\non_error: :stop ends the run and rethrows on wait; :skip logs a warning, counts the failure and continues.\nasync = true returns the TwinRunner immediately. Otherwise run! blocks until the source is exhausted, until is reached, or stop! is called.\n\nWhile a run is in progress, other tasks can still query the twin (state(twin), forecast(twin, …)), because every operation on the twin takes its lock.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Vardr.run!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Vardr.scenario-Tuple{Twin, Any, Real}","page":"API","text":"scenario(twin, interventions, tend; name = \"scenario\", kwargs...)\n\nForecast the twin under a list of AbstractInterventions (what-if analysis). Keywords are passed to forecast.\n\nlockdown = scenario(twin, [SetParameter(30.0, :β => 0.1)], 120.0; name = \"lockdown\")\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Vardr.scenario"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Vardr.snapshot-Tuple{AbstractString, Twin}","page":"API","text":"snapshot(path, twin)\n\nSave the twin's dynamic state (time, belief, fixed parameters, history and RNG state) to a JLD2 file. Model code and the observation function are not saved. Reconstruct the twin with the same code, then call restore!.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Vardr.snapshot"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Vardr.state-Tuple{Twin, Any}","page":"API","text":"Posterior mean of one state or estimated parameter, by name or index.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Vardr.state"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Vardr.state-Tuple{Twin}","page":"API","text":"Posterior mean of the model states.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Vardr.state"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Vardr.statenames-Tuple{TwinModel}","page":"API","text":"State names of the model (Symbols, or u1, u2, … when the model is unnamed).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Vardr.statenames"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Vardr.stop!-Tuple{TwinRunner}","page":"API","text":"stop!(runner)\n\nStop a running twin after the observation currently being processed, and wait for the run to finish.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Vardr.stop!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Vardr.uncertainty-Tuple{Twin, Any}","page":"API","text":"Posterior standard deviation of one state or estimated parameter.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Vardr.uncertainty"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Vardr.uncertainty-Tuple{Twin}","page":"API","text":"Posterior standard deviation of the model states.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Vardr.uncertainty"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#Vardr.History","page":"API","text":"mutable struct History\n\nRecord of the twin's belief after every prediction and assimilation.\n\nHistory is a Tables.jl column table with columns t, kind (:init, :predict or :update), then <name> (posterior mean) and <name>_std for every augmented state component. Turn it into a DataFrame with DataFrame(history(twin)).\n\nnames::Vector{Symbol}: Names of the augmented state components.\nt::Vector{Float64}\nkind::Vector{Symbol}\nmean::Vector{Vector{Float64}}\nstd::Vector{Vector{Float64}}\nobservations::Vector{Observation}: Observations that have been assimilated.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Vardr.History"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#Vardr.StateSelector","page":"API","text":"Observation function that picks state components (a linear h).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Vardr.StateSelector"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Vardr.draw-Tuple{Random.AbstractRNG, EnsembleBelief, Integer}","page":"API","text":"Return n equally weighted columns drawn from the belief (all of them, unweighted, when n equals the number of members of an EnsembleBelief).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Vardr.draw"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Vardr.ess-Tuple{ParticleBelief}","page":"API","text":"Effective sample size of a weighted belief.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Vardr.ess"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Vardr.getparam-Tuple{TwinModel, Any, Any}","page":"API","text":"Read parameter s from a parameter object (or anything with parameter_values).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Vardr.getparam"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Vardr.intervention_callbacks-Tuple{TwinModel, Any, Any}","page":"API","text":"Split interventions into those applied immediately (at or before t0) and a callback set (plus tstops) for the rest.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Vardr.intervention_callbacks"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Vardr.member_params-Tuple{Twin, Any}","page":"API","text":"Parameter object for a member with augmented state z.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Vardr.member_params"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Vardr.member_problem-Tuple{TwinModel, Any, Any, Any}","page":"API","text":"The model's problem with a member's state, parameters and time span.\n\nbuild_initializeprob = false stops ModelingToolkit from recomputing u0 from its Initial(...) parameters when an explicit parameter object is passed.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Vardr.member_problem"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Vardr.observed_mask-Tuple{Observation}","page":"API","text":"Indices of the non-missing entries of an observation.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Vardr.observed_mask"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#Vardr.predicted_observations","page":"API","text":"Predicted observations h(u, p, t) for every member, as columns.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Vardr.predicted_observations"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Vardr.propagate-Tuple{TwinModel, Vararg{Any, 4}}","page":"API","text":"Integrate u from t0 to t1 with parameters p and return the final state.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Vardr.propagate"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Vardr.solve_member-Tuple{TwinModel, Any}","page":"API","text":"Solve a member problem with the model's solver and keyword arguments.\n\ncallback and tstops are combined with any the model was built with (for example a dosing schedule), never substituted for them.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Vardr.solve_member"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Vardr.state_index-Tuple{TwinModel, Any}","page":"API","text":"Resolve a state reference (Int, Symbol, or symbolic variable) to its index in u.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Vardr.state_index"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Vardr.states_at-Tuple{Any, Any}","page":"API","text":"The saved state at each time in ts. Callbacks may save extra points (for example just before and after a dose), so look states up by time, taking the last value saved at each time (the post-event state).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Vardr.states_at"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#Vardr.systematic_resample","page":"API","text":"Systematic resampling: n indices drawn proportionally to normalized weights w.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Vardr.systematic_resample"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Vardr.tforeach-Tuple{Any, Integer}","page":"API","text":"Run f(i) for i in 1:n across threads. An exception from any iteration is rethrown as-is instead of wrapped in a CompositeException.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Vardr.tforeach"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Vardr.truncate!-Tuple{Vardr.History, Integer}","page":"API","text":"Drop history records after the first n (observations are kept).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Vardr.truncate!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Vardr.withparams-Tuple{TwinModel, Any, Any, Any}","page":"API","text":"Return a copy of the parameter object p with parameters syms set to vals.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Vardr.withparams"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/troubleshooting/#Troubleshooting","page":"Troubleshooting","text":"","title":"Troubleshooting"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/troubleshooting/#The-twin-ignores-the-data-(filter-divergence)","page":"Troubleshooting","text":"Symptoms: the estimate drifts away from the measurements while uncertainty is tiny.\n\nThe ensemble has become overconfident: with almost no spread, the update gives the data no weight. Fixes, roughly in order:\n\nIncrease process_noise on the states. The model is never perfect, so say so.\nUse EnKF(inflation = 1.02), up to about 1.1.\nUse more members (N).\nCheck the sensor noise σ. If it is set much smaller than the real noise, the twin chases noise; if much larger, it trusts data too little.","title":"The twin ignores the data (filter divergence)"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/troubleshooting/#A-parameter-estimate-freezes-or-wanders","page":"Troubleshooting","text":"Freezes at a wrong value with tiny uncertainty: the parameter ensemble collapsed. Give the parameter a small process_noise, and for a ParticleFilter a jitter.\nUncertainty never shrinks: the data does not inform this parameter. It is not identifiable from these sensors at this stage. Measure something else, fix it at a calibrated value, or wait for richer data (excitation).\nNoisy, wandering estimate: too much process_noise on the parameter. Reduce it.\nHits a physical limit: add bounds, and check that the prior covers the truth.","title":"A parameter estimate freezes or wanders"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/troubleshooting/#\"all-particle-weights-are-zero\"","page":"Troubleshooting","text":"Every particle is incompatible with the observation. The prior or state is far from the truth, the sensor noise is underestimated, or there is an outlier. Widen the prior, increase N, add jitter and process noise, check σ, or start with an EnKF, which does not have this failure mode.","title":"\"all particle weights are zero\""},{"category":"section","location":"guide/troubleshooting/#\"observation-at-t-…-but-the-twin-is-at-t-…\"","page":"Troubleshooting","text":"assimilate! needs obs.t == twin.t. Use step!, which predicts first. If the observation is older than the twin, it cannot be assimilated: sort your data, or use run!, which drops (and counts) late data.","title":"\"observation at t = … but the twin is at t = …\""},{"category":"section","location":"guide/troubleshooting/#\"cannot-predict-backwards\"","page":"Troubleshooting","text":"Time only moves forward. To re-run from an earlier point, restore! a snapshot or build a new twin.","title":"\"cannot predict backwards\""},{"category":"section","location":"guide/troubleshooting/#\"model-integration-failed-with-retcode-…\"","page":"Troubleshooting","text":"A member's ODE solve failed. Usually a member reached a nonphysical region (a negative concentration in a sqrt or log), or the problem is stiff:\n\nadd bounds to keep members physical;\nguard the right-hand side (sqrt(max(h, 0)));\nuse a stiff solver: TwinModel(prob; alg = Rodas5P());\nreduce an overly wide prior or process_noise.","title":"\"model integration failed with retcode …\""},{"category":"section","location":"guide/troubleshooting/#ModelingToolkit:-states-reset-to-their-initial-values","page":"Troubleshooting","text":"Vardr disables MTK's initialization when members are integrated. If you build problems yourself with remake, pass build_initializeprob = false and initializealg = SciMLBase.NoInit(), as Vardr.member_problem does.","title":"ModelingToolkit: states reset to their initial values"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/troubleshooting/#Names-not-found-(\"unknown-state\")","page":"Troubleshooting","text":"Plain-function models need states = [...] and params = [...] to use names.\nFor MTK models, both twin[I] and twin[:I] work for states. For parameters in SetParameter and calibrate!, pass the symbolic variable (β) or its Symbol.\nOnly parameters listed in estimate can be read with twin[...]. Use paramvalues for the others.","title":"Names not found (\"unknown state\")"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/troubleshooting/#It-is-slow","page":"Troubleshooting","text":"Start Julia with threads: julia -t auto.\nUse an in-place model function f!(du, u, p, t) for larger systems, and avoid allocations inside it.\nFewer members: an EnKF with localization often works with 20–50 members, even for hundreds of states.\nLooser solver tolerances (TwinModel(...; reltol = 1e-4)). Filter noise usually dominates integration error.\nForecast with fewer nsamples, and only the saveat points you need.\nThe first step! includes compilation time; measure the second.","title":"It is slow"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/troubleshooting/#Results-are-not-reproducible","page":"Troubleshooting","text":"Pass rng = Xoshiro(seed) to Twin (and optionally to forecast). The default global RNG is shared with everything else in the session.","title":"Results are not reproducible"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/tutorial/#Tutorial:-your-first-twin","page":"Tutorial","text":"This tutorial builds a complete digital twin step by step. The system is a damped oscillator (a mass on a spring) whose spring constant we do not know exactly:\n\nddot x = -kx - cdot x\n\nWe measure its position x with a noisy sensor. By the end, the twin will:\n\ntrack the full state (x and the unmeasured velocity v) from noisy measurements,\nlearn the spring constant k while it runs,\nforecast the future with uncertainty, and\nanswer a what-if question.\n\nEvery code block on this page runs when the documentation is built.","title":"Tutorial: your first twin"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/tutorial/#0.-Setup","page":"Tutorial","text":"using Vardr, Distributions, LinearAlgebra, Random, Statistics\n\nStart Julia with threads (julia -t auto): the twin integrates its ensemble members in parallel.","title":"0. Setup"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/tutorial/#1.-Some-\"real\"-data","page":"Tutorial","text":"In practice the data comes from your system. Here we simulate a real oscillator with k = 3.0 and add sensor noise (σ = 0.05). Vardr.propagate integrates a model from one time to another; we use it to create the ground truth.\n\nosc(u, p, t) = [u[2], -p[1] * u[1] - p[2] * u[2]]    # u = [x, v], p = [k, c]\n\nreal_system = TwinModel(osc, [1.5, 0.0], [3.0, 0.1])\ntruth(t) = Vardr.propagate(real_system, [1.5, 0.0], [3.0, 0.1], 0.0, t)\n\nrng = Xoshiro(42)\nmeasurements = [Observation(t, truth(t)[1] + 0.05 * randn(rng)) for t in 0.1:0.1:10.0]\nfirst(measurements, 3)\n3-element Vector{Observation}:\n Observation(0.1, [1.5170485624958803])\n Observation(0.2, [1.36749335464771])\n Observation(0.3, [1.2602819016108933])\n\nAn Observation is just a time and a vector of measured values.","title":"1. Some \"real\" data"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/tutorial/#2.-The-model","page":"Tutorial","text":"A TwinModel wraps the equations, the nominal initial state and the parameters. Naming states and parameters lets you refer to them as :x or :k everywhere. Our design value of the spring constant is k = 4.0, which is wrong.\n\nmodel = TwinModel(osc, [1.0, 0.0], [4.0, 0.1]; states = [:x, :v], params = [:k, :c])\nstatenames(model), paramnames(model)\n([:x, :v], [:k, :c])","title":"2. The model"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/tutorial/#3.-The-sensors","page":"Tutorial","text":"An ObservationModel describes what is measured and how noisy it is. Here one sensor reads x with standard deviation 0.05.\n\nsensors = ObservationModel(model, [:x], 0.05)","title":"3. The sensors"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/tutorial/#4.-The-twin","page":"Tutorial","text":"The Twin combines the model and sensors with a belief: an ensemble of possible states of the real system. Its key choices are:\n\nestimator: how to update the belief when data arrives. The EnKF (ensemble Kalman filter) is a robust default.\nestimate: parameters to learn online. They are appended to the state, so the twin works with z = [x, v, k].\nprior: what we believe before seeing data, over all of z.\nprocess_noise: how much the real system may drift away from the model per unit time. A small value on k keeps the twin able to learn.\n\nprior = MvNormal([1.0, 0.0, 4.0], Diagonal([0.5, 0.5, 1.0] .^ 2))  # over [x, v, k]\ntwin = Twin(model, sensors; name = \"oscillator\",\n    estimator = EnKF(N = 100), estimate = [:k], prior,\n    process_noise = [0.01, 0.01, 0.01], rng = Xoshiro(1))\nTwin(\"oscillator\", t = 0.0, EnKF with 100 members)","title":"4. The twin"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/tutorial/#5.-Synchronize-with-data","page":"Tutorial","text":"step! advances the twin to the observation's time (predict!) and then corrects it with the measurement (assimilate!):\n\nfor obs in measurements\n    step!(twin, obs)\nend\ntwin\nTwin(\"oscillator\", t = 10.0, EnKF with 100 members)\n\nQuery the twin by name. twin[:x] is the posterior mean and uncertainty the posterior standard deviation:\n\n(x = twin[:x], x_true = truth(twin.t)[1], v = twin[:v], v_true = truth(twin.t)[2])\n(x = 0.02619175191866638, x_true = 0.005841707079459621, v = 1.577293978463423, v_true = 1.5757985400341703)\n\n(k = twin[:k], k_std = uncertainty(twin, :k), k_true = 3.0)\n(k = 3.0176340201059673, k_std = 0.021042596361482955, k_true = 3.0)\n\nThe twin has learned k and the velocity, even though neither is measured. paramvalues lists all parameters, both estimated and fixed:\n\nparamvalues(twin)\n2-element Vector{Pair{Symbol, Float64}}:\n :k => 3.0176340201059673\n :c => 0.1","title":"5. Synchronize with data"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/tutorial/#6.-Look-at-the-history","page":"Tutorial","text":"Every prediction and update is recorded. history returns a Tables.jl table, so it converts directly to a DataFrame:\n\nusing DataFrames\ndf = DataFrame(history(twin))\nlast(df[df.kind .== :update, [:t, :x, :x_std, :k, :k_std]], 5)\n\u001b[1m5×5 DataFrame\u001b[0m\n\u001b[1m Row \u001b[0m│\u001b[1m t       \u001b[0m\u001b[1m x          \u001b[0m\u001b[1m x_std     \u001b[0m\u001b[1m k       \u001b[0m\u001b[1m k_std     \u001b[0m\n\u001b[1m     \u001b[0m│\u001b[90m Float64 \u001b[0m\u001b[90m Float64    \u001b[0m\u001b[90m Float64   \u001b[0m\u001b[90m Float64 \u001b[0m\u001b[90m Float64   \u001b[0m\n─────┼────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n   1 │     9.6  -0.584293   0.0176327  3.0108   0.021568\n   2 │     9.7  -0.450335   0.018269   3.00965  0.0214697\n   3 │     9.8  -0.301078   0.0182641  3.01068  0.0206733\n   4 │     9.9  -0.131819   0.0189758  3.01815  0.0206741\n   5 │    10.0   0.0261918  0.0179711  3.01763  0.0210426","title":"6. Look at the history"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/tutorial/#7.-Forecast","page":"Tutorial","text":"forecast propagates samples of the current belief into the future without changing the twin. Parameter uncertainty is carried along.\n\nfc = forecast(twin, 20.0; saveat = 10.0:0.5:20.0)\nTwinForecast(\"forecast\", t ∈ [10.0, 20.0], 100 samples)\n\nx_mean = mean(fc)[1, :]                 # states × times\nx_lo, x_hi = quantile(fc, 0.05)[1, :], quantile(fc, 0.95)[1, :]\nDataFrame(t = fc.t, mean = x_mean, lower = x_lo, upper = x_hi)[1:4:end, :]\n\u001b[1m6×4 DataFrame\u001b[0m\n\u001b[1m Row \u001b[0m│\u001b[1m t       \u001b[0m\u001b[1m mean       \u001b[0m\u001b[1m lower       \u001b[0m\u001b[1m upper      \u001b[0m\n\u001b[1m     \u001b[0m│\u001b[90m Float64 \u001b[0m\u001b[90m Float64    \u001b[0m\u001b[90m Float64     \u001b[0m\u001b[90m Float64    \u001b[0m\n─────┼──────────────────────────────────────────────\n   1 │    10.0   0.0261918  -0.00802042   0.0536288\n   2 │    12.0  -0.289875   -0.321124    -0.252429\n   3 │    14.0   0.47452     0.43851      0.508343\n   4 │    16.0  -0.574542   -0.603575    -0.543485\n   5 │    18.0   0.594514    0.573423     0.612447\n   6 │    20.0  -0.546757   -0.562116    -0.530246\n\nfc[:x] gives the raw sample trajectories (times × samples), which you can use to compute any probability you like. For example, the chance that |x| exceeds 0.85 at some point in the forecast:\n\nmean(maximum(abs.(fc[:x]); dims = 1) .> 0.85)\n0.5","title":"7. Forecast"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/tutorial/#8.-Ask-\"what-if?\"","page":"Tutorial","text":"A scenario is a forecast with interventions. What if we add a damper (c → 1.0) at t = 12?\n\nbaseline = forecast(twin, 20.0)\ndamped = scenario(twin, [SetParameter(12.0, :c => 1.0)], 20.0; name = \"damper at t = 12\")\nDataFrame(compare(baseline, damped; var = :x))\n\u001b[1m2×5 DataFrame\u001b[0m\n\u001b[1m Row \u001b[0m│\u001b[1m scenario         \u001b[0m\u001b[1m mean       \u001b[0m\u001b[1m std         \u001b[0m\u001b[1m lower      \u001b[0m\u001b[1m upper      \u001b[0m\n\u001b[1m     \u001b[0m│\u001b[90m String           \u001b[0m\u001b[90m Float64    \u001b[0m\u001b[90m Float64     \u001b[0m\u001b[90m Float64    \u001b[0m\u001b[90m Float64    \u001b[0m\n─────┼───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n   1 │ forecast          -0.546757   0.00956415   -0.562116   -0.530246\n   2 │ damper at t = 12  -0.0147926  0.000676936  -0.0157756  -0.0136788","title":"8. Ask \"what if?\""},{"category":"section","location":"guide/tutorial/#9.-Save-and-resume","page":"Tutorial","text":"path = snapshot(joinpath(mktempdir(), \"oscillator.jld2\"), twin)\n\ntwin2 = Twin(model, sensors; estimator = EnKF(N = 100), estimate = [:k], prior,\n    process_noise = [0.01, 0.01, 0.01])\nrestore!(twin2, path)\ntwin2[:k] == twin[:k]\ntrue","title":"9. Save and resume"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/tutorial/#Where-next","page":"Tutorial","text":"Models and sensors: ModelingToolkit models, inputs, callbacks, and custom measurement functions.\nState and parameter estimation: choosing and tuning estimators.\nForecasts and what-if scenarios: interventions and decision analysis.\nCalibration and persistence: offline fitting and snapshots.\nRunning live: streaming data, sinks and outages.\nTroubleshooting: what to do when the twin goes wrong.","title":"Where next"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/live/#Running-live","page":"Running live","text":"So far we have stepped twins through data in a loop. In operation, data arrives asynchronously from sensors, message brokers or databases. run! connects a twin to a data source and calls sinks after every update:\n\n source ──▶ run!(twin, source) ──▶ step!(twin, obs) ──▶ sinks (log, alert, save, publish)\n              ▲         │\n          WallClock     └─ other tasks may query the twin at any time","title":"Running live"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/live/#Replaying-recorded-data:-TableSource","page":"Running live","text":"TableSource turns any Tables.jl table (a DataFrame, CSV.File, or named tuple of vectors) into a stream. It is the easiest way to test a live setup against historical data.\n\nrng = Xoshiro(7)\nts = 0.1:0.1:10.0\ntable = (time = collect(ts), x = [truth(t)[1] + 0.05 * randn(rng) for t in ts])\n\ntwin = new_twin()\nrunner = run!(twin, TableSource(table; time = :time, columns = [:x]))\nTwinRunner(twin, finished, assimilated = 100, late = 0, rollbacks = 0)\n\ncolumns lists the measured columns, in the observation model's output order. missing values become NaN, meaning not observed.\nspeed: model time units per wall-clock second. The default Inf replays as fast as possible; speed = 60.0 replays one model minute per second, to rehearse real-time behaviour.\nRows are sorted by time first.\n\nWithout async = true, run! blocks until the source is exhausted.","title":"Replaying recorded data: TableSource"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/live/#Pushing-data-from-your-code:-ChannelSource","page":"Running live","text":"ChannelSource is the bridge to any data feed: a serial port, an MQTT or Kafka client callback, an HTTP handler, a database poller. Your code calls put!(src, Observation(t, y)); the twin runs in a background task.\n\nsrc = ChannelSource()\ntwin = new_twin()\nrunner = run!(twin, src; async = true)          # returns immediately\n\nfeed = Threads.@spawn for t in 0.1:0.1:5.0       # e.g. your sensor callback\n    put!(src, Observation(t, truth(t)[1] + 0.05 * randn()))\nend\nwait(feed)\nsleep(0.5)                                       # let the twin catch up\n(t = twin.t, x = twin[:x], k = twin[:k])         # query while it runs\n(t = 5.0, x = -0.8165774489069283, k = 3.0081938876576335)\n\nstop!(runner)     # or close(src) to end the stream gracefully\nTwinRunner(twin, finished, assimilated = 50, late = 0, rollbacks = 0)\n\nA sketch for MQTT (with Mosquitto.jl) looks like this; any client library follows the same pattern:\n\nclient = Mosquitto.Client(\"broker.local\")\nsubscribe(client, \"plant/line3/levels\")\n@async while true\n    msg = take!(get_messages_channel(client))\n    d = JSON3.read(msg.payload)\n    put!(src, Observation(d.t, [d.h1, something(d.h3, NaN)]))\nend\n\nwarning: Use model time, not arrival time\nObservation.t must be the time the measurement was taken, in the model's time units: for example hours since commissioning, (timestamp - t_ref) / 3600. Pick a reference time t_ref and use it everywhere.","title":"Pushing data from your code: ChannelSource"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/live/#Sinks:-reacting-to-updates","page":"Running live","text":"After every update, run! calls each sink with the twin and the observation (nothing after a clock tick). A CallbackSink wraps any function:\n\nlogger = CallbackSink() do twin, obs\n    obs !== nothing && obs.t % 2 == 0 &&\n        println(\"t = $(twin.t): k = $(round(twin[:k]; digits = 3)) ± $(round(uncertainty(twin, :k); digits = 3))\")\nend\n\nalerts = Tuple{Float64, Float64}[]\nalarm = CallbackSink() do twin, obs\n    obs === nothing && return\n    fc = forecast(twin, twin.t + 2.0; nsamples = 50)\n    p = mean(maximum(fc[:x]; dims = 1) .> 0.9)     # P(x > 0.9 within 2 time units)\n    p > 0.5 && push!(alerts, (twin.t, p))          # or @warn, send an e-mail, …\nend\n\ntwin = new_twin()\nrun!(twin, TableSource(table; time = :time); sinks = [logger, alarm])\nlength(alerts), first(alerts, 3)\n(58, [(0.1, 1.0), (0.2, 1.0), (0.3, 1.0)])\n\nTypical sinks:\n\nwrite the state to a time-series database or dashboard;\npublish the estimate back to the control system;\nraise alerts from forecasts (see examples/thermal_room_twin.jl);\nsave snapshots with SnapshotSink(path; every = 100), which also saves at shutdown.\n\nSinks run in the runner's task, so a slow sink delays the twin. Hand heavy work (long forecasts, network calls) to another task, for example through a Channel.","title":"Sinks: reacting to updates"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/live/#Writing-your-own-sink","page":"Running live","text":"Subtype AbstractSink, implement on_update, and optionally Base.close, which is called once when the run ends:\n\nmutable struct CSVSink <: AbstractSink\n    io::IO\nend\nfunction Vardr.on_update(s::CSVSink, twin, obs)\n    println(s.io, twin.t, \",\", twin[:x], \",\", uncertainty(twin, :x))\nend\nBase.close(s::CSVSink) = flush(s.io)\n\nbuf = IOBuffer()\nrun!(new_twin(), TableSource(table; time = :time); sinks = [CSVSink(buf)])\nfirst(split(String(take!(buf)), '\\n'), 3)\n3-element Vector{SubString{String}}:\n \"0.1,1.445232066714106,0.05040106149086059\"\n \"0.2,1.470138826654151,0.037350412487211895\"\n \"0.3,1.2857452518101349,0.033171068387766324\"","title":"Writing your own sink"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/live/#Writing-your-own-source","page":"Running live","text":"Subtype AbstractDataSource and implement Vardr.start(src), which returns a Channel{Observation}. If the source creates the channel itself, also define Vardr.owns_channel(src) = true, so that stop! can close it.\n\nstruct PollingSource <: AbstractDataSource\n    url::String\n    every::Float64            # seconds\nend\nVardr.owns_channel(::PollingSource) = true\nfunction Vardr.start(src::PollingSource)\n    Channel{Observation}(64) do ch\n        while true\n            r = JSON3.read(HTTP.get(src.url).body)\n            put!(ch, Observation(r.t, [r.temperature, r.humidity]))\n            sleep(src.every)\n        end\n    end\nend","title":"Writing your own source"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/live/#Outages-and-the-wall-clock","page":"Running live","text":"If sensors go quiet, the twin would otherwise stop at the last observation time. A WallClock keeps it moving: after interval seconds of silence, it predicts dt model time units ahead and notifies the sinks with obs = nothing. The uncertainty grows during the outage, honestly reflecting that the twin is \"flying blind\".\n\nrun!(twin, src; clock = WallClock(interval = 60.0, dt = 1/60), async = true)\n#                                  1 min of silence → advance 1 min (time in hours)\n\nSet dt to the model time that passes in interval seconds of real time.\n\nClock ticks are provisional. If a delayed observation then arrives with a time the ticks have already passed, the twin rolls back to the state before the ticks and assimilates it, so no data is lost. runner.rollbacks counts these events.","title":"Outages and the wall clock"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/live/#Late-and-out-of-order-data","page":"Running live","text":"A twin only moves forward in time. In a live run:\n\nSituation What happens\nObservation newer than the twin predict to it, then assimilate (normal case)\nOlder, but only provisional ticks passed it roll back, assimilate (runner.rollbacks += 1)\nOlder than the last assimilated observation dropped (runner.late += 1)\nSame time as the twin assimilated (several sensors at one time)\n\nIf your feed is often out of order, buffer it briefly and sort before put!.","title":"Late and out-of-order data"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/live/#Errors,-stopping,-and-the-runner","page":"Running live","text":"runner = run!(twin, source; sinks, until = Inf, clock = nothing,\n              on_error = :stop, async = false)\n\nuntil: stop once model time reaches this value.\non_error = :stop (default): an exception while assimilating ends the run, and is rethrown by wait(runner). on_error = :skip logs a warning, counts it in runner.failed, and continues. Use :skip in production, together with monitoring of runner.failed.\nstop!(runner): finish the current observation, then stop. Sinks are closed.\nwait(runner): block until the run ends. istaskdone(runner) checks without blocking.\n\nThe TwinRunner counts assimilated, late, ticks, rollbacks and failed. Printing it gives a one-line status.","title":"Errors, stopping, and the runner"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/live/#Concurrency","page":"Running live","text":"Every operation on a twin (step!, forecast, state, snapshot, …) takes the twin's lock, so a dashboard, REST endpoint or REPL can query a running twin safely. Forecasts release the lock after drawing their samples, so a long what-if analysis does not block assimilation.\n\nStart Julia with several threads (julia -t auto). Ensemble members are integrated in parallel, and a sensor thread, the runner and your queries can run side by side.","title":"Concurrency"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/live/#Inputs-that-arrive-live","page":"Running live","text":"Known inputs that change during operation, such as setpoints, valve positions or doses given, are part of the model's forcing, not observations. Keep a time-stamped log that the model reads, and append to it as changes arrive:\n\nconst valve_log = [(0.0, 0.5)]                         # (time, opening)\nvalve(t) = valve_log[findlast(e -> e[1] <= t, valve_log)][2]\ntank(u, p, t) = [p[1] * valve(t) - p[2] * sqrt(max(u[1], 0))]\n\non_valve_change(t, opening) = lock(twin.lock) do       # from the control system\n    push!(valve_log, (t, opening))\nend\n\nTaking the twin's lock while you append means no integration ever sees a half-updated log. Record changes before (or with) the sensor data that follows them.","title":"Inputs that arrive live"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/live/#A-production-checklist","page":"Running live","text":"Validate the twin on synthetic data, then replay historical data with TableSource(...; speed = ...).\nUse a fixed time reference, and model-time timestamps on every observation.\nUse on_error = :skip, a WallClock if outages matter, and a SnapshotSink.\nOn restart, rebuild the twin from code, restore! the last snapshot, and resume the feed from twin.t.\nMonitor runner.late, runner.failed and the twin's uncertainty. A twin whose uncertainty keeps growing despite data, or whose innovations are biased, has a model that no longer matches the system. That is often the most valuable alarm of all.","title":"A production checklist"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/models/#Models-and-sensors","page":"Models and sensors","text":"A twin needs two descriptions of the real system:\n\na process model, the TwinModel: how the state evolves, du/dt = f(u, p, t);\na measurement model, the ObservationModel: what the sensors see, y = h(u, p, t) + ε with ε ~ N(0, R).","title":"Models and sensors"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/models/#Process-models","page":"Models and sensors","text":"","title":"Process models"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/models/#From-a-plain-Julia-function","page":"Models and sensors","text":"Write the right-hand side out-of-place, f(u, p, t) -> du, or in-place, f!(du, u, p, t). In-place is faster for larger systems.\n\nlv(u, p, t) = [p[1] * u[1] - p[2] * u[1] * u[2],     # prey\n               p[3] * u[1] * u[2] - p[4] * u[2]]      # predator\n\nmodel = TwinModel(lv, [10.0, 5.0], [1.1, 0.4, 0.1, 0.4];\n    states = [:prey, :predator], params = [:α, :β, :δ, :γ])\nTwinModel(2 states [:prey, :predator], 4 parameters, Tsit5)\n\nu0 and p are the nominal values. The twin's initial belief is centred on them unless you give an explicit prior.\nstates and params are optional, but names make every other call more readable (twin[:prey], SetParameter(t, :β => 0.3)). Without names, use indices: twin[1], SetParameter(t, 2 => 0.3).\nt0 sets the model's start time (default 0.0).\np can be any vector. Parameters are read and written through SymbolicIndexingInterface, so they keep their position in p.","title":"From a plain Julia function"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/models/#From-ModelingToolkit","page":"Models and sensors","text":"Any ODEProblem works, including one built with ModelingToolkit. States and parameters are then referred to by their symbolic variables, or by Symbol:\n\nusing ModelingToolkit\nusing ModelingToolkit: t_nounits as t, D_nounits as D\n\n@parameters β γ\n@variables S(t) I(t) R(t)\n@mtkcompile sir = System([D(S) ~ -β * S * I,\n                          D(I) ~ β * S * I - γ * I,\n                          D(R) ~ γ * I], t)\nprob = ODEProblem(sir, [S => 0.99, I => 0.01, R => 0.0, β => 0.5, γ => 0.1], (0.0, 1.0))\nmodel = TwinModel(prob)\n\nsensors = ObservationModel(model, [I], 0.002)\ntwin = Twin(model, sensors; estimate = [β])\ntwin[I] == twin[:I]                       # both forms work\n\nThe tspan of the problem is irrelevant: only its start time is used. The twin chooses the integration interval at each step.\n\nnote: Initialization\nModelingToolkit problems normally run an initialization system that recomputes u0 whenever parameters change. A twin's members already carry consistent states, so TwinModel disables this (initializealg = NoInit()). If your model has algebraic constraints (a DAE), make sure your prior respects them.","title":"From ModelingToolkit"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/models/#Solver-and-solver-options","page":"Models and sensors","text":"The default solver is Tsit5(), which is good for non-stiff problems. Pass any other SciML algorithm and solve keywords:\n\nusing OrdinaryDiffEqRosenbrock\nmodel = TwinModel(prob; alg = Rodas5P(), abstol = 1e-8, reltol = 1e-6)\n\nUse a stiff solver for chemical kinetics, detailed thermal networks or other models with widely separated time scales. If an integration fails, predict! throws, and the belief is left unchanged.","title":"Solver and solver options"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/models/#Time-varying-inputs","page":"Models and sensors","text":"Known inputs (weather, a control schedule, pump demand) are functions of t inside f:\n\noutdoor(t) = 5.0 + 4.0 * sin(2π * (t - 9) / 24)\nheater(t)  = 6 <= mod(t, 24) < 22 ? 1.0 : 0.0\nroom(u, p, t) = [((outdoor(t) - u[1]) / p[1] + p[2] * heater(t)) / p[3]]\nmodel = TwinModel(room, [18.0], [5.0, 2.0, 0.5]; states = [:T], params = [:R, :P, :C],\n    tstops = sort(vcat(6:24:240, 22:24:240)))   # stop at the switching times\n\nWhen an input jumps, add the jump times as tstops, so the solver does not step over them.\n\nInputs that only become known live, such as an actuator setting changed by an operator, can be read from a log that your input feed appends to. See Inputs that arrive live.","title":"Time-varying inputs"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/models/#Discrete-events:-callbacks","page":"Models and sensors","text":"Callbacks and tstops given to TwinModel are applied to every integration of every member, and also in forecasts and calibration. They are combined with Vardr's own callbacks, never replaced by them. A typical use is a dosing schedule:\n\nusing SciMLBase: DiscreteCallback\ndose_times = 0.0:12.0:240.0\ndosing = DiscreteCallback((u, t, integ) -> t in dose_times,\n    integ -> (integ.u[1] += integ.p[8]))        # p[8] = dose amount\nmodel = TwinModel(pk, u0, p; states, params, callback = dosing, tstops = dose_times)\n\nSee examples/pkpd_patient_twin.jl for a complete version.","title":"Discrete events: callbacks"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/models/#Large-and-spatial-models","page":"Models and sensors","text":"For a discretized PDE, write an in-place f! over the grid and name the cells. For example, states = [Symbol(:T, i) for i in 1:40]. The twin works the same way. With many unknowns, see Localization in the estimation guide.","title":"Large and spatial models"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/models/#Measurement-models","page":"Models and sensors","text":"","title":"Measurement models"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/models/#Sensors-that-read-states-directly","page":"Models and sensors","text":"The most common case: give the states that are measured and their noise standard deviations (a scalar or one per sensor).\n\nsensors = ObservationModel(model, [:prey, :predator], [0.5, 0.3])\nsensors.names, sensors.R\n([:prey, :predator], [0.25 0.0; 0.0 0.09])","title":"Sensors that read states directly"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/models/#A-custom-measurement-function","page":"Models and sensors","text":"When the sensors measure a function of the state, pass h(u, p, t) and the noise. The noise can be a vector of standard deviations or a full covariance matrix R (for correlated sensor errors).\n\n# total population, and the predator fraction\nh(u, p, t) = [u[1] + u[2], u[2] / (u[1] + u[2])]\nsensors = ObservationModel(h, [1.0, 0.02]; names = [:total, :predator_fraction])\n\n# correlated errors\nR = [1.0 0.01; 0.01 0.02^2]\nsensors = ObservationModel(h, R; names = [:total, :predator_fraction])\n\nh receives each member's parameters, including any that are being estimated. A sensor may therefore depend on unknown parameters, such as a calibration gain or a drug's potency.\n\ntip: Keep `h` cheap and pure\nh is evaluated once per member per observation. Do not mutate u or p inside it.","title":"A custom measurement function"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/models/#Missing-values","page":"Models and sensors","text":"An Observation always has one entry per output, in the order of the observation model. Mark unavailable readings with NaN; they are skipped in the update:\n\nObservation(12.0, [3.4, NaN])     # only the first sensor reported\n\nAn observation where every value is NaN is a no-op. This makes it easy to merge sensors that report at different rates into one stream.","title":"Missing values"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/models/#Units-and-scaling","page":"Models and sensors","text":"Vardr does not rescale anything. Keep states, parameters and measurements in units where their magnitudes are not wildly different, for example hours instead of seconds, or mg/L instead of g/m³. This helps both the ODE solver and the estimator's covariance computations.","title":"Units and scaling"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/models/#Simulation-only-twins","page":"Models and sensors","text":"A twin without an observation model is a stochastic simulator. It is useful for Monte Carlo studies, or to generate synthetic \"real\" data:\n\nsim = Twin(model; estimator = NoAssimilation(N = 50))   # 10% spread around nominal values\nfc = forecast(sim, 20.0)\nround.(mean(fc)[:, end]; digits = 2)\n2-element Vector{Float64}:\n 9.59\n 1.59","title":"Simulation-only twins"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/#Examples","page":"Examples","text":"The repository's examples/ directory contains complete, runnable twins. Each script simulates a \"real system\" with known ground truth, builds a twin from imperfect prior knowledge, and reports how well the twin recovers the truth. They are a good template for your own twin.\n\njulia --project=examples -e 'using Pkg; Pkg.develop(path=\".\"); Pkg.instantiate()'\njulia --project=examples -t auto examples/thermal_room_twin.jl\n\nExample System Features shown Guide pages\noscillator_twin.jl Damped oscillator Joint state–parameter EnKF, forecast, scenario, compare Tutorial\nsir_epidemic_twin.jl SIR epidemic (ModelingToolkit) Symbolic models, TableSource replay, interventions Models and sensors, Running live\nlorenz63_twin.jl Lorenz-63 (chaotic) Synchronizing from one observed variable; forecast skill vs ensemble spread State and parameter estimation, Forecasts and what-if scenarios\npkpd_patient_twin.jl Two-compartment PK/PD Dosing callbacks, sparse mixed measurements (NaN), nonlinear h with estimated parameters, regimen comparison Models and sensors, Forecasts and what-if scenarios\nthermal_room_twin.jl 2R2C building with a thermostat Live ChannelSource, alert sinks, SnapshotSink, WallClock outages with rollback, retrofit what-ifs Running live\nheat_rod_twin.jl 1-D heat equation, 40 cells High-dimensional twin, covariance localization Localization\ntank_leak_detection_twin.jl Three-tank process (ModelingToolkit) Fault detection, unmeasured states, abrupt parameter change, decision support State and parameter estimation, Forecasts and what-if scenarios","title":"Examples"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/#Building-your-own-twin:-a-recipe","page":"Examples","text":"Model. Write f(u, p, t), or an MTK system, with named states and parameters. Simulate it and check that it behaves plausibly.\nSensors. List what is measured, at what noise level. Mark gaps with NaN.\nSynthetic test. Choose \"true\" parameters that differ from your design values, simulate noisy data, and check that the twin recovers the truth. Compare it against NoAssimilation.\nTune. Set the prior, process_noise, N and inflation until the errors are small and the uncertainty is honest (~95% of the truth inside ±2σ).\nCalibrate on historical data with calibrate!, if you have it.\nReplay history through run! with a TableSource, with sinks.\nGo live with a ChannelSource or your own source, a WallClock and a SnapshotSink.\nUse it. Add forecasts, alerts and what-if analyses for the decisions that matter.","title":"Building your own twin: a recipe"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/whatif/#Forecasts-and-what-if-scenarios","page":"Forecasts and what-if scenarios","text":"Once a twin is synchronized with the real system, it can answer two kinds of questions without touching the real system:\n\n\"What happens next?\": a forecast from the current belief.\n\"What if we do X?\": a scenario, which is a forecast with interventions.\n\nNeither changes the twin: they work on samples drawn from its belief, so you can call them at any time, even while the twin runs live.\n\nThe examples below use the oscillator twin from the Tutorial, synchronized up to t = 10.","title":"Forecasts and what-if scenarios"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/whatif/#Forecasts","page":"Forecasts and what-if scenarios","text":"forecast(twin, tend; saveat, nsamples, interventions = [], name = \"forecast\", rng)\n\ntend: forecast end time (must be after twin.t).\nsaveat: output times (default: 101 points from twin.t to tend).\nnsamples: number of trajectories (default: the number of members). For a particle filter, samples are drawn according to the weights.\n\nEach sample starts from one member and keeps that member's estimated parameters, so the forecast spread includes both state and parameter uncertainty. Process noise is not added, so forecasts show the uncertainty in what the twin knows, not future random disturbances.\n\nfc = forecast(twin, 15.0; saveat = 10.0:0.25:15.0)\nTwinForecast(\"forecast\", t ∈ [10.0, 15.0], 100 samples)","title":"Forecasts"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/whatif/#Working-with-a-[TwinForecast](@ref)","page":"Forecasts and what-if scenarios","text":"Call Shape Meaning\nfc.t ntimes output times\nfc.names nstates state names\nmean(fc), std(fc) nstates × ntimes ensemble mean and standard deviation\nquantile(fc, q) nstates × ntimes e.g. q = 0.05, 0.95 for a 90% band\nfc[:x] ntimes × nsamples sample trajectories of one state\nfc.samples nstates × ntimes × nsamples everything\n\nThe sample trajectories turn any question into a probability. Write the condition per sample, then average:\n\nX = fc[:x]\np_exceed = mean(any(abs.(X) .> 0.85; dims = 1))      # P(|x| > 0.85 at some time)\nt_first_peak = [fc.t[argmax(X[:, k])] for k in axes(X, 2)]\n(p_exceed = p_exceed, peak_time = mean(t_first_peak), peak_time_std = std(t_first_peak))\n(p_exceed = 0.61, peak_time = 10.8375, peak_time_std = 0.11984312136027557)\n\nTo plot a forecast band with any plotting package:\n\nusing CairoMakie\nlo, hi = quantile(fc, 0.05)[1, :], quantile(fc, 0.95)[1, :]\nband(fc.t, lo, hi; alpha = 0.3); lines!(fc.t, mean(fc)[1, :])","title":"Working with a TwinForecast"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/whatif/#Interventions","page":"Forecasts and what-if scenarios","text":"Intervention Effect at time t\nSetParameter(t, :c => 1.0, :k => 2.5) change one or more parameters\nSetState(t, :v => 0.0) overwrite one or more states\nEvent(t, integ -> ...) anything else: an arbitrary affect!(integrator)\n\nAn intervention at or before the forecast start is applied immediately.\nSetParameter on an estimated parameter overrides every sample's value, so its uncertainty is gone after t.\nCombine any number of interventions in a vector. They are applied in time order.\n\nplans = [\n    forecast(twin, 20.0; name = \"do nothing\"),\n    scenario(twin, SetParameter(12.0, :c => 1.0), 20.0; name = \"add damper at 12\"),\n    scenario(twin, [SetState(11.0, :v => 0.0), SetParameter(11.0, :c => 0.5)], 20.0;\n        name = \"brake at 11\"),\n    scenario(twin, Event(12.0, integ -> (integ.u[1] *= 0.5)), 20.0;\n        name = \"halve x at 12\"),\n]\nDataFrame(compare(plans...; var = :x, t = 15.0))\n\u001b[1m4×5 DataFrame\u001b[0m\n\u001b[1m Row \u001b[0m│\u001b[1m scenario         \u001b[0m\u001b[1m mean     \u001b[0m\u001b[1m std        \u001b[0m\u001b[1m lower    \u001b[0m\u001b[1m upper    \u001b[0m\n\u001b[1m     \u001b[0m│\u001b[90m String           \u001b[0m\u001b[90m Float64  \u001b[0m\u001b[90m Float64    \u001b[0m\u001b[90m Float64  \u001b[0m\u001b[90m Float64  \u001b[0m\n─────┼────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n   1 │ do nothing        0.463396  0.0246369   0.426369  0.504288\n   2 │ add damper at 12  0.171348  0.00474435  0.163002  0.179044\n   3 │ brake at 11       0.284205  0.0061151   0.273903  0.294908\n   4 │ halve x at 12     0.51985   0.0192372   0.4903    0.550899\n\nEvent gives full access to the SciML integrator: integ.u, integ.p, integ.t. Use it for a pulse input, a dose, or a valve that closes. Modify integ.u and integ.p in place.","title":"Interventions"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/whatif/#Recurring-actions","page":"Forecasts and what-if scenarios","text":"For actions that repeat (a dosing regimen, a daily schedule), generate the list:\n\nregimen(amount) = [Event(t, integ -> (integ.u[1] += amount)) for t in 12.0:12.0:72.0]\nscenario(twin, regimen(150.0), 72.0; name = \"150 mg q12h\")\n\nIf the intervention changes an input that the model reads from a parameter, a sequence of SetParameters is simpler.","title":"Recurring actions"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/whatif/#Comparing-options","page":"Forecasts and what-if scenarios","text":"compare summarizes one state at one time across forecasts. It returns a Tables.jl table with the columns scenario, mean, std, lower and upper:\n\ncompare(fcs...; var = :x, t = nothing, quantiles = (0.05, 0.95))\n\nReal decisions usually need a custom metric that combines the whole trajectory: total throughput, time in a target range, or risk of exceeding a limit. Compute it per sample from fc[...], then summarize:\n\n# time (per sample) during which |x| < 0.2\ntime_calm(fc) = vec(sum(abs.(fc[:x]) .< 0.2; dims = 1)) .* (fc.t[2] - fc.t[1])\nDataFrame(option = [fc.name for fc in plans],\n          calm_time = [mean(time_calm(fc)) for fc in plans],\n          P_calm_over_3 = [mean(time_calm(fc) .> 3) for fc in plans])\n\u001b[1m4×3 DataFrame\u001b[0m\n\u001b[1m Row \u001b[0m│\u001b[1m option           \u001b[0m\u001b[1m calm_time \u001b[0m\u001b[1m P_calm_over_3 \u001b[0m\n\u001b[1m     \u001b[0m│\u001b[90m String           \u001b[0m\u001b[90m Float64   \u001b[0m\u001b[90m Float64       \u001b[0m\n─────┼────────────────────────────────────────────\n   1 │ do nothing            1.885            0.0\n   2 │ add damper at 12      6.3              1.0\n   3 │ brake at 11           5.442            1.0\n   4 │ halve x at 12         1.949            0.0\n\nThe examples pkpd_patient_twin.jl (dosing regimens: efficacy against toxicity) and tank_leak_detection_twin.jl (throughput against overflow risk) show this pattern in full.","title":"Comparing options"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/whatif/#Good-practice","page":"Forecasts and what-if scenarios","text":"Use the same nsamples and a fixed rng for every option in a comparison. The differences then reflect the interventions, not sampling noise. With the default nsamples (the number of members), an EnKF forecast uses every member exactly once.\nForecast skill is limited. Uncertainty grows with the horizon, much faster in chaotic systems (see examples/lorenz63_twin.jl). Report the spread, not just the mean.\nModel what can change. A scenario can only change what the model represents. If an option acts on something that is hard-coded, make it a parameter.","title":"Good practice"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/calibration/#Calibration-and-persistence","page":"Calibration and persistence","text":"","title":"Calibration and persistence"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/calibration/#Offline-calibration-vs-online-estimation","page":"Calibration and persistence","text":"Vardr offers two ways to learn parameters:\n\n calibrate! (offline) estimate = [...] (online)\nData a batch of historical observations one observation at a time\nMethod maximum likelihood, by optimization Bayesian filtering\nResult point estimates, written into the twin's fixed parameters a posterior that evolves\nUse for commissioning: fitting a new twin to historical data following a system as it runs and drifts\n\nThey work well together: calibrate most parameters once on history, then estimate the few that drift online.","title":"Offline calibration vs online estimation"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/calibration/#calibrate!","page":"Calibration and persistence","text":"calibrate!(twin, data, params; alg, lb = nothing, ub = nothing,\n           adtype = SciMLBase.NoAD(), u0 = state(twin), solve_kwargs...)\n\nIt simulates the model from u0 at twin.t through all data, and minimizes the Gaussian negative log-likelihood under the twin's observation model. Missing (NaN) values are ignored.\n\nusing OptimizationOptimJL: NelderMead\n\ntwin = Twin(model, sensors; estimator = EnKF(N = 50), rng = Xoshiro(1))\nsol = calibrate!(twin, measurements, [:k, :c]; alg = NelderMead(), u0 = [1.5, 0.0])\nsol.u, paramvalues(twin)\n([3.0015025967187885, 0.099258201817321], [:k => 3.0015025967187885, :c => 0.099258201817321])\n\nparams: parameter names (or indices) to fit; the others stay fixed.\nalg: any Optimization.jl optimizer. NelderMead() needs no gradients. For gradient-based optimizers (LBFGS()), pass adtype = AutoForwardDiff(), and make sure your model function accepts dual numbers (avoid Float64[...] literals inside f).\nlb, ub: bounds, for optimizers that support them.\nu0: the initial state of the simulation. It defaults to the twin's current mean state. If the initial state is uncertain too, include it in your own loss, or start the data a little later and let the filter handle it.\nExtra keywords (maxiters = 500) go to Optimization.solve.\n\nAfter calibration, the fitted values become the twin's fixed parameters. If a calibrated parameter is also in estimate, its members are shifted so that their mean equals the fitted value, keeping their spread.\n\nAll observations must be at or after twin.t. Calibrate a fresh twin on history, then assimilate newer data.","title":"calibrate!"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/calibration/#Snapshots","page":"Calibration and persistence","text":"A twin that runs for weeks must survive restarts. snapshot saves its dynamic state to a JLD2 file: time, belief, fixed parameters, history and RNG state.\n\ntwin = Twin(model, sensors; estimator = EnKF(N = 50), estimate = [:k],\n    process_noise = 0.01, rng = Xoshiro(1))\nforeach(o -> step!(twin, o), measurements[1:50])\npath = snapshot(joinpath(mktempdir(), \"twin.jld2\"), twin)\n\"/tmp/jl_dCS3yD/twin.jld2\"\n\nCode is not saved: the model function, observation function and estimator settings belong in your source code. To restore, build the twin with the same code, then load the state:\n\nbuild() = Twin(model, sensors; estimator = EnKF(N = 50), estimate = [:k],\n    process_noise = 0.01, rng = Xoshiro(0))\nrestored = restore!(build(), path)\nforeach(o -> step!(restored, o), measurements[51:end])\nforeach(o -> step!(twin, o), measurements[51:end])\nrestored[:k] == twin[:k]          # continues exactly where it left off\ntrue\n\nrestore! checks that the snapshot fits the twin: the same state dimension and the same belief type. With an explicit RNG of the same type (Xoshiro), the restored twin continues bit-for-bit identically.\n\nWhile running live, SnapshotSink writes snapshots periodically and at shutdown. See Running live.","title":"Snapshots"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/estimation/#State-and-parameter-estimation","page":"State and parameter estimation","text":"A twin's belief is a cloud of N members: possible states of the real system. Each time data arrives, the twin:\n\npredicts: integrates every member up to the observation time and adds process noise (predict!);\nupdates: moves or reweights the members towards what the sensors saw (assimilate!).\n\nstep!(twin, obs) does both. The posterior mean is the twin's best guess, and the spread is its uncertainty.","title":"State and parameter estimation"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/estimation/#Building-the-twin:-every-keyword-explained","page":"State and parameter estimation","text":"Twin(model, sensors;\n     estimator     = EnKF(N = 100),\n     estimate      = [:k],\n     prior         = MvNormal(μ, Σ),\n     process_noise = [0.01, 0.01, 0.001],\n     bounds        = (lower, upper),\n     t0            = 0.0,\n     rng           = Xoshiro(1),\n     name          = \"pump-7\")","title":"Building the twin: every keyword explained"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/estimation/#estimate:-joint-state-and-parameter-estimation","page":"State and parameter estimation","text":"Parameters listed in estimate are appended to the state. The twin then works with the augmented state z = [u; θ], here [x, v, k], and every member carries its own value of k. Parameters that are not listed stay fixed at the model's values; read them with paramvalues.\n\nEstimate a parameter when it is uncertain and the data can inform it. Estimating too many parameters from too few sensors gives wide, correlated posteriors. Check with uncertainty whether the twin is actually learning.","title":"estimate: joint state and parameter estimation"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/estimation/#prior:-what-you-believe-at-the-start","page":"State and parameter estimation","text":"The prior is over the whole augmented state, in the order [states...; estimated...].\n\nprior Meaning\nnothing (default) Gaussian, mean = nominal u0 and parameter values, 10% standard deviation\na Distributions distribution e.g. MvNormal(μ, Σ), or product_distribution([Uniform(...), ...])\na matrix your own members, one per column (size = (nz, N))\n\nMake the prior wide enough to contain the truth. An ensemble cannot learn what it never considers, and a particle filter in particular cannot recover from a prior that misses the truth.","title":"prior: what you believe at the start"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/estimation/#process_noise:-how-wrong-the-model-may-be","page":"State and parameter estimation","text":"The standard deviation, per unit of time, of a random walk added to each component of z after every prediction. It is a scalar, or one value per component.\n\nOn states: it represents model error and unmodelled disturbances. Too little, and the twin becomes overconfident and stops listening to data (the filter diverges). Too much, and the estimates become noisy.\nOn estimated parameters: a small value keeps the parameter ensemble from collapsing to a point, and lets the twin follow slow drift in the real system, such as wear, fouling or a leak that grows. Use a larger value if parameters can change abruptly.\n\nIt scales with √dt, so the same setting works for irregular sampling.","title":"process_noise: how wrong the model may be"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/estimation/#bounds:-physical-limits","page":"State and parameter estimation","text":"(lower, upper), each a scalar or a vector over z. Members are clamped after every prediction and update. Use it for quantities that cannot be negative (concentrations, water levels, resistances):\n\nbounds = (vcat(zeros(3), 0.0), fill(Inf, 4))","title":"bounds: physical limits"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/estimation/#rng,-t0,-name","page":"State and parameter estimation","text":"rng: pass a seeded RNG (Xoshiro(1)) for reproducible runs and exact snapshot round trips.\nt0: start time of the belief (default: the model's start time).\nname: shown when printing the twin and in runner logs.","title":"rng, t0, name"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/estimation/#Choosing-an-estimator","page":"State and parameter estimation","text":" EnKF ParticleFilter NoAssimilation\nUpdate Shifts members (linear-Gaussian assumption) Reweights and resamples members None (open loop)\nTypical N 20–200 500–10,000 any\nDimension Scales to thousands of states (with localization) Small (≲ 10 unknowns) any\nGood for Most twins; mildly nonlinear systems; many sensors Strong nonlinearity, multimodal or non-Gaussian beliefs, bounded or discrete-like states Simulation, baselines, synthetic data\nMain risk Ensemble collapse (use inflation and process noise) Weight degeneracy (use jitter, a larger N, a wider prior) n/a\n\nStart with the EnKF. Move to the particle filter only when the EnKF's Gaussian assumption clearly fails, for example when the posterior is bimodal.","title":"Choosing an estimator"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/estimation/#EnKF-tuning","page":"State and parameter estimation","text":"EnKF(N = 100, inflation = 1.02, localization = nothing)\n\nN: more members give better covariance estimates. Cost grows linearly.\ninflation: spreads the ensemble by this factor before each update. Values of 1.01–1.1 counteract the EnKF's tendency to become overconfident with small ensembles.\nlocalization: see Localization below.","title":"EnKF tuning"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/estimation/#Particle-filter-tuning","page":"State and parameter estimation","text":"ParticleFilter(N = 2000, resample_threshold = 0.5, jitter = 0.05)\n\nresample_threshold: resample when the effective sample size drops below this fraction of N.\njitter: after resampling, perturbs each particle by this fraction of the posterior standard deviation. This prevents all particles from collapsing onto a few copies, which matters most for estimated parameters (they have no dynamics of their own to spread them out).\n\npf = Twin(model, sensors; estimator = ParticleFilter(N = 2000, jitter = 0.05),\n    estimate = [:k], prior = MvNormal([1.0, 0.0, 4.0], Diagonal([0.5, 0.5, 1.0] .^ 2)),\n    process_noise = [0.01, 0.01, 0.005], rng = Xoshiro(3))\nforeach(o -> step!(pf, o), measurements)\n(k = pf[:k], k_std = uncertainty(pf, :k))\n(k = 3.005988957495002, k_std = 0.01395380973911183)","title":"Particle filter tuning"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/estimation/#Localization","page":"State and parameter estimation","text":"With fewer members than unknowns (common for spatial models), the EnKF's sample covariances contain spurious correlations: a sensor at one end of a pipe seems to \"inform\" the other end. Localization tapers covariances to zero with distance.\n\nEnKF(N = 20, localization = (Lxy, Lyy))\n\nLxy (nstates × noutputs): the taper between each model state and each sensor.\nLyy (noutputs × noutputs): the taper between sensors.\n\nBuild both from the same correlation function of distance, typically the Gaspari–Cohn function. See the heated-rod example (examples/heat_rod_twin.jl) for a complete version:\n\nL(i, j) = gaspari_cohn(abs(position[i] - position[j]) / c)   # c ≈ half the cut-off distance\nLxy = [L(i, s) for i in 1:nstates, s in sensor_states]\nLyy = [L(s, r) for s in sensor_states, r in sensor_states]\n\nIf the sensors observe states directly (ObservationModel(model, vars, σ)), passing Lxy alone is enough, and Lyy is derived from it. Estimated parameters are global, so they always use the unlocalized update.","title":"Localization"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/estimation/#Stepping-manually","page":"State and parameter estimation","text":"step! is predict! followed by assimilate!. Call them separately when you need to:\n\ntwin = Twin(model, sensors; estimator = EnKF(N = 100), estimate = [:k],\n    prior = MvNormal([1.0, 0.0, 4.0], Diagonal([0.5, 0.5, 1.0] .^ 2)),\n    process_noise = 0.01, rng = Xoshiro(1))\n\npredict!(twin, 0.5)                 # advance without data (e.g. a missed reading)\nobs = Observation(0.5, truth(0.5)[1] + 0.05 * randn(rng))\nassimilate!(twin, obs)              # requires obs.t == twin.t\ntwin.t\n0.5\n\nRules:\n\nTime only moves forward: predict!(twin, t) with t < twin.t throws. Data older than the twin cannot be assimilated (sort your data first; the live runner handles this for you).\nSeveral observations at the same time can be assimilated one after another.\npredict! without a later assimilate! is how a twin \"coasts\" through outages. Its uncertainty grows, which is exactly what you want.","title":"Stepping manually"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/estimation/#Reading-the-belief","page":"State and parameter estimation","text":"Call Returns\ntwin[:x], state(twin, :x) posterior mean of a state or estimated parameter\nstate(twin) mean of all model states\nuncertainty(twin, :x), uncertainty(twin) posterior standard deviation\nparamvalues(twin) name => value for every parameter\nmembers(twin) the member matrix (nz × N), for any custom statistic\nhistory(twin) every prediction and update (a Tables.jl table)\ntwin.t current model time\n\nMembers let you compute anything, for example correlations between unknowns or the probability of an event now:\n\nforeach(o -> step!(twin, o), measurements[6:end])\nZ = members(twin)\n(corr_x_k = cor(Z[1, :], Z[3, :]), P_k_above_3 = mean(Z[3, :] .> 3.0))\n(corr_x_k = 0.5056050483882678, P_k_above_3 = 0.71)\n\nFor a ParticleFilter the members are weighted. Use mean, std and cov of twin.belief, or Vardr.weights(twin.belief), rather than unweighted statistics of members(twin).","title":"Reading the belief"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/estimation/#Is-the-twin-working?-Diagnostics","page":"State and parameter estimation","text":"Test your twin on synthetic data first: simulate a \"real\" system with known parameters, as every example in examples/ does. Then check:\n\nAccuracy: the error of the posterior mean against the truth should be well below that of an open-loop run (NoAssimilation).\nCalibration of the uncertainty: the truth should lie within ±2σ about 95% of the time. If it is much less, the twin is overconfident: increase process_noise, inflation or N. If it is always far inside, the twin is too cautious.\nParameter learning: plot k and k_std from the history. The mean should settle and the uncertainty shrink. If the uncertainty stays at its prior value, the data does not inform that parameter.\n\nusing DataFrames\nh = DataFrame(history(twin))\nupd = h[h.kind .== :update, :]\nerr = [abs(r.x - truth(r.t)[1]) / r.x_std for r in eachrow(upd)]  # error in units of σ\n(within_1σ = mean(err .< 1), within_2σ = mean(err .< 2),           # ideal ≈ 0.68 and 0.95\n final_k = upd.k[end], final_k_std = upd.k_std[end])\n(within_1σ = 0.875, within_2σ = 1.0, final_k = 3.0127532668545887, final_k_std = 0.02041486769539546)\n\nHere the twin is somewhat cautious: the truth lies within 1σ more often than the ideal 68%. That is the safe side to err on. Reducing process_noise on the states would tighten the uncertainty.\n\nFor real data without ground truth, look at the innovations: the difference between each measurement and the twin's prediction just before it. They should look like zero-mean noise, with a spread that matches the predicted uncertainty plus the sensor noise.","title":"Is the twin working? Diagnostics"},{"category":"section","location":"#Vardr.jl","page":"Home","text":"Vardr is a general-purpose framework for building digital twins on top of SciML.\n\nA twin in Vardr has four parts:\n\nA TwinModel: any ODEProblem, for example one generated by ModelingToolkit, plus the solver used to advance it.\nAn ObservationModel: what the sensors measure, y = h(u, p, t) + ε.\nA belief about the real system's augmented state z = [u; θ]: the model states u and any parameters θ estimated online. It is represented by an ensemble (EnsembleBelief) or weighted particles (ParticleBelief).\nAn estimator that keeps the belief synchronized with data: EnKF, ParticleFilter, or NoAssimilation (open loop).\n\ndata ──▶ step!(twin, obs) = predict!(twin, obs.t) + assimilate!(twin, obs)\n                                 │                          │\n                    integrate every member        update belief with obs\n                                 └──────── twin.belief ─────┘\n                                               │\n                    forecast · scenario · compare · calibrate! · snapshot","title":"Vardr.jl"},{"category":"section","location":"#Where-to-start","page":"Home","text":"New to Vardr? Follow the Tutorial: one twin, built step by step, in about 10 minutes.\nBuilding your own twin? See Models and sensors and State and parameter estimation, then the recipe on the Examples page.\nUsing a twin for decisions: Forecasts and what-if scenarios.\nDeploying: Running live and Calibration and persistence.\nSomething wrong: Troubleshooting.","title":"Where to start"},{"category":"section","location":"#Installation","page":"Home","text":"Vardr is not yet registered. Install it from GitHub:\n\nusing Pkg\nPkg.add(url = \"https://github.com/natapol/Vardr.jl\")\n\nYou will usually also want Distributions (for priors), and ModelingToolkit if you write models symbolically.","title":"Installation"},{"category":"section","location":"#The-twin-lifecycle","page":"Home","text":"Stage Functions\nBuild TwinModel, ObservationModel, Twin\nSynchronize predict!, assimilate!, step!\nQuery state, uncertainty, paramvalues, twin[:x], history\nWhat-if forecast, scenario with SetParameter, SetState, Event; compare\nCalibrate calibrate!\nOperate live run! with an AbstractDataSource and AbstractSinks; stop!\nPersist snapshot, restore!","title":"The twin lifecycle"},{"category":"section","location":"#Tips","page":"Home","text":"Start Julia with threads (julia -t auto). Ensemble members are integrated in parallel.\nGive estimated parameters a small process_noise, so their ensemble spread does not collapse and the twin can follow slow drift in the real system.\nParticle filters need a prior that covers the truth and many more members than an EnKF.\nUse an explicit RNG (rng = Xoshiro(seed)) for reproducible runs and exact snapshot/restore! round trips.","title":"Tips"}]
}
